    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Где мой сушист?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where is my sushi chef?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'where' directly with 'sushi chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Откуда я знаю?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How should I know?' Senya combines 'where from' with 'know' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Утром он был здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He was here this morning.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'this morning' with 'here'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Может, он заболел.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe he got sick.' The verb for 'to fall ill' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Суши отменяются?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is sushi canceled?' Kristina links 'sushi' directly with 'to be canceled' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ничего не отменяется.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nothing is canceled.' Notice the reflexive ending on the verb for 'is canceled', which directs the action back onto the subject. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "У нас восемь заказов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have eight orders.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'at' directly with 'orders'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам всё сделаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will do everything myself.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'I'll do'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Клиенты ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Customers are waiting.' Notice how 'client' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'are waiting'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Идите отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get out of here.' Notice how 'come' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'from here'. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Мы уже уходим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We are already leaving.' Senya links 'we' directly with 'already' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Думай быстрее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Think fast.' Kristina combines 'think' with 'faster' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
